---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import NavBar from '../components/NavBar.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Mission" description="amber mission and vision, AI on Linux, unleashed">
  <NavBar />
  <main>
    <h1>Mission &amp; Vision</h1>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>The North Star</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        AI on Linux, unleashed.
      </p>
      <p>
        amber is a single agentic tool for the terminal: one tool, one interface,
        one mental model. It writes, edits, refactors, and reviews code. It manages
        servers, runs commands, and monitors systems. It searches codebases, reads
        docs, and manages itself, sessions, config, providers, jobs. It persists:
        leave it running for days and come back to results.
      </p>
      <p>
        Linux is the armour: stability, longevity, tool integration, the entire UNIX
        philosophy. AI is the brain. Other harnesses stack Python, Node, or hosted
        SaaS layers on top of that. amber is compiled C++17, one process, one
        binary, no interpreter, no account, no telemetry.
      </p>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider-thick" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Mission</h2>
      <p>
        Build a bare-metal C++17 agent harness for Linux that reads, writes,
        searches, runs commands, and compresses context across arbitrarily long
        sessions, engineered for stability, benchmarked with real data, and
        trusted with a single fix or an entire project.
      </p>
      <p>
        The agent runs until it is done. Background jobs survive. You drive it with
        commands; it reports back in the scrollback. Every action has a command
        path; every command produces UI feedback. Help is one <code>?</code> away
        at any depth, you never retype a path.
      </p>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>The Spirit</h2>
      <p>
        God-like power wrapped in a terminal, delivered through an interface
        inspired by BitchX and the golden age of IRC. Fast, keyboard-driven,
        dense, beautiful. The drawer pops on <code>/</code>, filters as you type,
        and <code>/q</code>, <code>/sl</code> become muscle memory again. The
        interface anticipates. It helps. It gets out of your way when you know
        what you are doing.
      </p>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Who It Is For</h2>
      <ul class="feature-list">
        <li><strong>Developers</strong>, from a single file to a monorepo</li>
        <li><strong>System administrators</strong>, server management, automation, deployment</li>
        <li><strong>Security professionals</strong>, auditing, tooling, long-running investigations</li>
        <li><strong>Researchers</strong>, data analysis, experiments that run for days</li>
        <li><strong>Anyone</strong> who SSHes into a box and wants an AI partner</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        amber is free and open source (Apache 2.0), for everyone, without
        discrimination. If you live in a terminal, it is yours.
      </p>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>What We Stand For</h2>
      <table class="stand-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>We are</th>
            <th>We are not</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Terminal-native</td><td>A web app</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Keyboard-first</td><td>Mouse-driven</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Local-first, self-hosted</td><td>SaaS or telemetry</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Linux-powered</td><td>A Windows or macOS port</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Composable with UNIX tools</td><td>A walled garden</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Insert-always</td><td>A modal editor</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Compiled, integrated</td><td>A plugin platform</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .section p.lead {
    color: #00ff41;
    font-size: 1.4em;
    font-weight: bold;
  }

  .stand-table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 13px;
    margin: 16px 0;
  }

  .stand-table th,
  .stand-table td {
    padding: 8px 16px;
    text-align: left;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #333333;
  }

  .stand-table th {
    color: #00ff41;
    font-weight: bold;
    border-bottom: 2px solid #333333;
  }

  .stand-table td:first-child {
    color: #00ff41;
    width: 55%;
  }

  .stand-table td:last-child {
    color: #888888;
  }
</style>
